id: terrify
name: Terrify
type: standard
actionCost: 1
kiCost: 0
attacking: false
requiresTarget: true
special: true
usageLimit: 1/round
clashSkill: intimidation
clashDefenderSkills: intuition, intimidation
terrify: true
source: Core Rule
description: >
  Frighten an Opponent within 4 Squares. A Clash of your Intimidation against their
  Intuition or Intimidation; winning leaves them Shaken until the end of your next turn,
  and knocks them Prone if they were Shaken already. Punching up costs you 2 on the roll.
text: >
  Terrify Maneuver [1/Round]: Intimidate an opponent into submission.
  –Maneuver Type: Standard Maneuver
  –Action Cost: 1 Action
  –Minions: N/A
  –KP Cost: N/A
  –Exploitable: N/A
  –Effect: Target an Opponent within 4 Squares of you. Make a Skill Clash (Intimidation
  vs Intuition/Intimidation) against them. If you win, your target gains the Shaken
  Combat Condition until the end of your next turn. If they already possessed the Shaken
  Combat Condition, they are additionally knocked Prone.

  Reduce the Dice Score of your Skill Clash by 2 if your target is of a higher Tier of
  Power than you.

---

# --- The Clash ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "(Intimidation vs Intuition/Intimidation)". The slash is a choice made by whoever is
# rolling it, as it is in the Feint's "(Bluff vs Intuition/Perception)" - so the defender is
# asked which of the two before either side sees a number.
#
# The second option is the challenger's own Skill, which the Magic Trick's "(Use Magic vs
# Intuition/Use Magic)" also does: you may stare back instead of reading them. Nothing here
# needed changing for that - the two lists are separate and always were.
#
# A tie goes to the Defender, here as everywhere else.
#
# No script. What it leaves is a Condition on somebody else on a clock kept by you, and a
# passive writes Slots onto the character holding it.
#
# --- Punching up --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "Reduce the Dice Score of your Skill Clash by 2 if your target is of a higher Tier of
# Power than you."
#
# Its own paragraph, outside the Effect, so it applies whether or not the Clash is won -
# which it must, since it is what decides that.
#
# Yours and not theirs. The sentence is addressed to whoever used the Maneuver and "your
# target" is who they aimed it at, so it is a row on the challenger's side and the defender
# never takes it. A defender answering with Intimidation against somebody above their own
# Tier gets nothing for it: the sentence is about this Maneuver's user.
#
# Strictly higher. Equal Tiers are not higher, which is what the word says and is also the
# reading that does not quietly penalise a fair fight.
#
# The current Tier of Power on both sides, not the Base: (T) is what "Tier of Power" means
# everywhere in these rules, and a Transformation or a Holding Back Stack changes who is
# above whom. Which is worth noticing - somebody holding their power back is easier to
# frighten, and that follows from the two rules rather than from anything here.
#
# Applied where a Clash builds the side that is rolling, beside Intuit's bonus, because
# that is the only place that knows both who is rolling and who they are rolling against.
#
# --- What winning leaves ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "Your target gains the Shaken Combat Condition until the end of your next turn." Your
# turn, so the clock is kept by you and the Condition sits on them - the same split Analysis
# and Intuit use, and the entry that times it is also the record of who frightened whom.
#
# Shaken has no stacks: its entry is a flat -2(T) on Strike Rolls and a rule about moving
# towards whoever inflicted it. So a second Terrify does not stack it, and the second clock
# is a second entry rather than a replacement - one running out must not take the Condition
# off while the other is still counting.
#
# "If they already possessed the Shaken Combat Condition, they are additionally knocked
# Prone." Already, so it is read before this Maneuver writes it: the Shaken this use is
# about to apply must not be what counts as having had it. That ordering is the whole of the
# rule and is the one thing here that a reasonable build gets backwards.
#
# Prone gets no clock. It is not given one by this entry and it has none of its own - it
# comes off the way Prone always comes off.
#
# --- What is not built ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "Within 4 Squares of you." Said and not policed, like every range in this system: nothing
# here measures a distance. It is on the card so the table can see the number it is being
# asked to agree about.
#
# "You cannot willingly use any effect to move towards the Character that inflicted this
# Combat Condition" - Shaken's own first half, which its file already records as unbuilt.
# Nothing records who inflicted a Condition, and this system moves nobody. Worth saying
# here because Terrify is the first Maneuver that inflicts it, so it is the first time that
# gap has anybody standing in it: the clock this leaves does name who did it, and it is not
# what that rule would need - it times the Condition rather than attaching the Character to
# it, and it is gone when the Condition is.
#
# --- How it is gained -----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Not known. Intimidation's entry has not been given, so no Skill in the table opens this
# yet.
#
# Intimidation is the obvious guess and the guess is worth nothing: the Dirty Trick is a
# Clash made with Bluff and Bluff opens the Feint instead. Being the Skill a Maneuver rolls
# and being the Skill that hands it over are two different questions, and only the Skill's
# own entry answers the second.
